window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"20-05","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"20-05-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-01/","title_he":"א – איסור עבודה זרה לבני נח","title_en":"The Prohibition of Idolatry for Bnei Noaḥ","body_en":"<p>Idolatry is forbidden to all human beings, for there is no transgression that diverts a person's mind from truth to falsehood and from good to evil more than idolatry. It is therefore agreed that it is the first mitzva, which Adam, the first man, was already commanded.</p><p>The law for a ben Noaḥ (a descendant of Noah, i.e. a non-Jew) is like the law for a Jew: one who worships idols is liable to death. However, as we have learned (above 3:2), the main intent of this is to express the severity of the sin under the laws of Heaven, but in practice the attitude toward people who committed this sin is complex. For just as there are mitzvot whose punishment is death, such as idolatry, so there are many mitzvot that earn a person life, and the overall reckoning for each person is made in the Heavenly Court. And just as God desires the good of all His creatures, so it is fitting for the righteous to do good to idol worshippers as well and to draw them closer to faith in God. This is how Avraham Avinu acted: he related to everyone with love and sought out guests to bring them into his home, honour them with food and drink and draw them closer to faith, even though in his days everyone worshipped idols. He acted this way even toward lowly idol worshippers who worshipped the dust of their feet (Rashi, Bereishit 18:4). This is also evident from the fact that he pleaded in defence of the people of Sodom, who, in addition to all their grave sins, certainly also worshipped idols.<sup>1</sup></p><p>In the definition of worship, too, the law for Bnei Noaḥ is like the law for Jews: the grave prohibition whose punishment is death is to declare acceptance of the idol as a god, or to worship it in the manner of worship particular to it, or in one of the four important services that were performed in the Temple, which are: 1) bowing down; 2) slaughtering a sacrifice; 3) burning a sacrifice on the altar; 4) pouring a libation of wine, or sprinkling blood and the like, in honour of the idol.</p><p>As for Jews, so for a ben Noaḥ it is forbidden to express honour or affection for an idol even when this is done not in the manner of worship customary for that idol, such as hugging the statue, kissing it or sweeping before it. But one is not liable to death for this. Likewise, it is forbidden for a ben Noaḥ to make a statue, but one who makes it is not liable to death (Sanhedrin 56b and Meiri there; Rambam, Hilkhot Melakhim 9:2; above 3:3).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"It is stated in the Talmud (Sanhedrin 56b): \"Rabbi Yehuda says: Adam, the first man, was commanded only regarding idolatry… Rabbi Yehuda ben Beteira says: also regarding blessing the Name,\" that is, not to offend the honour of Heaven with a curse. \"And some say: also regarding laws (dinim).\" The prevalent opinion is that Adam was commanded six mitzvot: 1) not to worship idols; 2) not to \"bless\" (curse) the Name; 3) not to murder; 4) not to commit adultery; 5) not to rob; 6) to appoint judges. After the Flood and the saving of the animals by Noah, people were permitted to eat the meat of animals, and then a mitzva related to the suffering of animals was added: not to eat a limb from a living animal. These are the seven mitzvot of Bnei Noaḥ (Sanhedrin 56b; Rambam, Hilkhot Melakhim 9:1).<br>For all seven mitzvot of Bnei Noaḥ one is liable to death; however, the intent is not that this must be carried out in practice. Rather, it comes to teach the severity of the transgression: when it is committed in grave defiance, the judges, with the consent of their nation, are permitted to execute the offender for it. This is as we learned above (3:2) regarding those liable to death in Israel, that the death penalty was carried out only rarely, mainly against murderers, since their sin involved a severe blow to faith and to national consensus. The main purpose of mentioning the death penalty for the various sins is to express the severity of the prohibition under the laws of Heaven. See Tosafot, Avoda Zara 64b, s.v. \"Eizehu,\" who explained regarding a ger toshav (resident alien) that even though he transgressed the seven mitzvot and seemingly became liable to death, as long as he has not been sentenced to death, there is a mitzva to sustain him (see further Tosafot, Avoda Zara 26b, s.v. \"Ve-lo moridin\"; Amud Ha-yemini 16)."},{"n":2,"en":"Some explained that all expressions of honour to idols are forbidden out of doubt, for in Sanhedrin 56b there is a dispute about what is included in the prohibition beyond the idol's regular worship: whether making a statue, or worship not in its usual manner and not in one of the four services of the Temple, is also included. Out of doubt it was ruled that everything is forbidden, but one is not liable to death for it (so explained Arukh La-ner, Sanhedrin 56b; Likutei Halakhot of the Ḥafetz Ḥayim there; Or Same'aḥ, Hilkhot Melakhim 9:2). Others explain that the doubt in Sanhedrin concerns liability to death, but it is agreed that there is a general prohibition on all Bnei Noaḥ against honouring idols or making them (Binyan Shlomo, Sanhedrin 56, s.v. \"Hineh\"; Ḥemdat Yisrael, Kuntres Ner Mitzva, negative commandment 2, s.v. \"U-lefi zeh\"; She'elat Shlomo 21; Igrot Moshe, YD 2:55, s.v. \"U-mi-ta'am\"). Included in the grave prohibition of idolatry, it is also forbidden for a non-Jew to declare that he accepts an idol as a god, and the implication is that this is at the level of liability to death (Taz, YD 148:3; Nekudot Ha-kesef there; Minḥat Ḥinukh 86:9). Below, in sections 8-10, we will learn that according to most poskim, idolatry in shituf (association of other powers with God) is permitted to Bnei Noaḥ, and accordingly all the prohibitions mentioned here apply when they do not associate the Name of Heaven."}]},{"slug":"20-05-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-02/","title_he":"ב – בין ישראל לאומות העולם באמונה וקידוש השם","title_en":"Between Israel and the Nations in Faith and Kiddush Hashem","body_en":"<p>Although all human beings are commanded to believe in God and not to worship idols, the mitzva of faith obligates Israel more than the other nations. For Israel were chosen by God to reveal His light and blessing in the world, and for this purpose He made a covenant with them at Mount Sinai and gave them the Torah, and warned them about faith in His unity in a special way. Thus it is stated (Devarim 6:4-5): \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is one. And you shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your might.\" \"With all your soul – even if He takes your soul\" (Mishna Berakhot 9:5; above 4:1).</p><p>Therefore the people of Israel are commanded regarding kiddush Hashem (sanctification of God's Name): if a Jew is coerced to worship idols, he is obligated to be killed rather than worship them, whereas a ben Noaḥ is not obligated to give up his life for this, and he is permitted to worship rather than be killed. As it is stated (Vayikra 22:31): \"You shall not profane My holy Name, and I shall be sanctified in the midst of the children of Israel.\" The Sages said: \"Israel are commanded regarding kiddush Hashem, and the non-Jews are not commanded regarding kiddush Hashem\" (Yerushalmi, Sanhedrin 3:5). Therefore, if a Jew is coerced to bow down to a statue, even in private, he must be killed rather than transgress. Not only that, but even if he is coerced to give honour to an idol in a manner that is not its main worship, such as hugging or kissing it, he must be killed rather than transgress (above 4:4). But if a ben Noaḥ is coerced, even in public, to bow down to a statue and worship it, he need not give up his life, for as long as he continues to believe in God in his heart, he may outwardly worship the idol and not be killed (Rambam, Hilkhot Melakhim 10:2; Tosafot and Ran).</p><p>We also learn this from the blessing of Elisha the prophet to Na'aman, the general of the army of Aram. Na'aman suffered from tzara'at, and after he immersed seven times in the Jordan as Elisha the prophet instructed, he recognised \"that there is no God in all the earth except in Israel\" (II Melakhim 5:15), and he took upon himself to serve God and to refrain from idolatry. However, he went on to say to Elisha apologetically that when he went with his king to the idol worship in the house of Rimon, he would be forced to bow down with him to the statue. Elisha blessed him, \"Go in peace,\" implying that he bore no prohibition, since he was doing so under duress. As it is stated (ibid. 5:17-19): \"And Na'aman said… for your servant will no longer offer burnt offering or sacrifice to other gods, but only to the Lord. In this matter may the Lord pardon your servant: when my master (my king) comes into the house of Rimon (idolatry) to bow down there, and he leans on my hand, and I bow down in the house of Rimon – when I bow down in the house of Rimon, may the Lord [please] pardon your servant in this matter. And he said to him: Go in peace.\"<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"In the Yerushalmi (Sanhedrin 3:5) and in the Bavli (there 74b), the question is raised whether Bnei Noaḥ are obligated to give up their lives for kiddush Hashem. In the Yerushalmi the answer is clear – they are not obligated, and the proof is from Na'aman. In the Bavli, however, there are two versions. According to the version of Tosafot (and so incline Rabbeinu Yona and Ran), the conclusion of the Bavli too is that they are not obligated to give up their lives, and so ruled Rambam (Hilkhot Melakhim 10:2) and Meiri. But according to the version of Rashi and Ramah (Rabbi Meir Abulafia), when the idolatry is done in the presence of ten Jews, it remains in doubt whether they must give up their lives. And according to Ran (Sanhedrin 75a), following their version, the doubt applies even in the presence of ten Bnei Noaḥ who believe in God's unity. But in private, he wrote in the name of Ramban, all agree that they are not obligated to give up their lives, for only Israel, who were commanded \"And you shall love the Lord your God… and with all your soul,\" were commanded to give up their lives for faith even in private.<br>Maharash Yaffe (Yefeh To'ar on Bereishit Rabba 44:5) wrote that Bnei Noaḥ need not give up their lives even regarding bloodshed. So wrote Pri Megadim (Teivat Gome, Shemot 1) and Arukh Ha-shulḥan He-atid (Melakhim 80:4). This is also implied by the Rishonim who wrote that Bnei Noaḥ need not give up their lives for any of the seven mitzvot (Rambam, Hilkhot Melakhim 10:2; Meiri, Sanhedrin 74b). On the other hand, according to Parashat Derakhim (Derush 2) and Arukh La-ner (Sanhedrin there), when coerced to murder, everyone is obligated to give up his life and not kill, since the basic reasoning that the other's blood is no less red than one's own (\"What makes you think [your blood is redder]?\") obligates everyone (see above 4:17). Indeed, Minḥat Ḥinukh (296:28) explained that where the reasoning of \"What makes you think\" applies, one is obligated to give up one's life, but where that reasoning does not apply, for example when they say, \"Either you kill so-and-so or we will kill all of you,\" they need not give up their lives and be killed. End quote. It is possible that all the Rishonim who wrote that one need not give up one's life for the seven mitzvot agree with this distinction: only when it is clear that his fellow will be killed [anyway] need he not give up his life, but when it is not clear, a ben Noaḥ is forbidden to murder his fellow in order to save himself. This is the main view. So wrote Rav Neria (Yisrael Bi-medinato, Ma'arkhot Ha-mishpat Ba-medina 6).<br>Although a ben Noaḥ is not obligated to give up his life, the implication is that if he wishes, he may give up his life for the sanctity of the Name. So wrote Ḥemdat Shlomo (OḤ 38:31), Yad Sha'ul (YD 345) and Meshekh Ḥokhma (Devarim 34:8). However, according to Maharit (Tzofnat Pa'ane'aḥ, Toldot, Derush 1), only Jews are permitted to give up their lives."}]},{"slug":"20-05-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-03/","title_he":"ג – ההבדל במקדש","title_en":"The Difference in the Temple","body_en":"<p>From a Jewish apostate, that is, a Jew who worships idols, a sacrifice is not accepted, but from a ben Noaḥ, even if he worships idols, a sacrifice is accepted. As it is stated (Vayikra 1:2): \"When a man of you brings an offering,\" and the Sages expounded (Ḥullin 5a): \"'Of you' – but not all of you, to exclude the apostate. 'Of you' – among you I made a distinction, but not among the nations,\" meaning that from members of the nations a sacrifice is accepted even when they worship idols. It is also stated (ibid. 17:8): \"Any man of the house of Israel, or of the strangers who sojourn among them, who offers a burnt offering,\" and the Sages expounded (Ḥullin 13b): \"'Any man' (ish ish) – to include idolaters, who vow vows and freewill offerings like Israel.\" (The sacrifices accepted from non-Jews are burnt offerings; Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aseh Ha-korbanot 3:2-4.)</p><p>Similarly, we learn that when King Shlomo finished building the Temple, he prayed to God to rest His Presence upon the Temple, and asked that when Israel come to pray in the Temple, God answer their prayer according to what they deserve by their deeds and the purity of their hearts. As it is stated (I Melakhim 8:38-39): \"Whatever prayer, whatever supplication there may be from any man of all Your people Israel… then hear in heaven, Your dwelling place, and forgive and act, and give to each man according to all his ways, whose heart You know.\" But when a non-Jew comes to pray before God, Shlomo asked that God always hear his prayer, so that he would be strengthened in his faith. As it is stated (ibid. 8:41-43): \"Also the foreigner who is not of Your people Israel, and comes from a distant land for Your Name's sake… and comes and prays toward this house – hear in heaven, Your dwelling place, and do according to all that the foreigner calls to You for, so that all the peoples of the earth may know Your Name, to fear You as do Your people Israel, and to know that Your Name is called upon this house that I have built.\" The commentators explained that Shlomo asked that God answer the prayer of non-Jews even though their hearts are not whole in their faith, and even if they were idol worshippers, in order to draw them closer (Rashi, Radak, Abarbanel, Alshikh, Malbim).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-05-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-04/","title_he":"ד – מצוות ביעור עבודה זרה ואיסור הנאה","title_en":"The Mitzva to Eradicate Idolatry and the Prohibition of Benefit","body_en":"<p>The people of Israel are commanded to eradicate idolatry from the Land of Israel, and every individual Jew, whether in the Land or abroad, is likewise commanded to eradicate idolatry that has come into his possession (above 3:9-10). However, Bnei Noaḥ have no obligation to eradicate idolatry from their land, nor is an individual obligated to eradicate idolatry that has come into his possession.</p><p>A Jew is forbidden to derive benefit from an idol, from its service vessels or from the offerings brought to it; he is even forbidden to sell it and benefit from the proceeds. Rather, he is commanded to destroy it from the world (below 10:1-2). Bnei Noaḥ, however, not only have no mitzva to destroy an idol, but are even permitted to benefit from the proceeds of an idol and of everything associated with it. Thus it was instructed regarding one who was about to convert, that so as not to lose the value of his statues, he should sell them and receive the money for them while he is still a non-Jew, and afterwards convert (Avoda Zara 64a).<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"So wrote Or Same'aḥ (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 9:11) and Meshekh Ḥokhma (Shemot 34:16). This is also the view of Turei Even (Ḥagiga 13a); Maharia Ha-levi (2:93); Minḥat Ḥinukh (428:1; 436:2), Ḥemdat Yisrael, Maharil Diskin (Kuntres Aḥaron 141), and others. And although Ramban and Ran (Avoda Zara 59b) imply that Bnei Noaḥ are forbidden to benefit from idolatry, so as not to contradict the Gemara, it is possible to explain that in their view a ben Noaḥ is forbidden to benefit from the idol itself, but is permitted to sell it and benefit from the proceeds.<br>According to Meshekh Ḥokhma (Devarim 7:5-6), a ger toshav too is not obligated to destroy idolatry, for this mitzva is incumbent upon Israel, who are called \"a holy people.\" Just as Israel are forbidden to eat [an animal] without ritual slaughter because they are called a holy people, whereas \"the stranger who is within your gates\" is permitted to eat it (Devarim 14:21). End quote. So wrote Divrei Yatziv (EH 19:15), but he added that in order to receive the status of ger toshav in the Land, he must also accept not to worship idols in shituf. So wrote Rav Avraham Price, as cited below in note 12.<br>Likewise, the prohibition \"You shall not bring an abomination into your house\" (Devarim 7:26), according to which a Jew is forbidden to bring an idol into his house (below 11:9), does not apply to a non-Jew (Leḥem Setarim by Rabbi Shlomo Algazi, Avoda Zara 15a; Minḥat Yitzḥak 1:120:3). Some say that a ben Noaḥ too is forbidden to bring an idol into his house (Ḥemdat Yisrael, Bnei Noaḥ, letter 5, s.v. \"Ve-hineh yesh\"). It seems that if there is a concern that he will thereby stumble into idolatry, he must refrain from this so as not to sin, in the way that Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Melakhim 9:2) that Bnei Noaḥ should not do things through which they are liable to stumble into idolatry."}]},{"slug":"20-05-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-05/","title_he":"ה – איסורים מיוחדים לישראל","title_en":"Prohibitions Specific to Israel","body_en":"<p>As we learned (3:5), the Torah warned Israel that one must not entice his fellow to worship idols. It was stringent in his punishment: even if in practice he did not succeed in persuading his fellow to worship idols, he is liable to death. It further warned not to pity him or have mercy on him, both in bringing him to court and in the court proceedings. However, a ben Noaḥ who enticed someone to worship idols, although he thereby transgressed a grave prohibition, is not liable to death, since the law of the mesit (enticer) does not apply to Bnei Noaḥ.<sup>5</sup></p><p>As a safeguard to the prohibition of idolatry, the Torah commanded Israel not to make images of a human being, the sun, the moon, the stars and the ministering angels. This Torah prohibition applies even when there is no concern that they will want to worship them (below 15:1-7). Bnei Noaḥ, however, are not forbidden to make them; only when there is a concern that they will stumble into worshipping them is it forbidden for them to make them.</p><p>Likewise, in order to distance Israel from any trace of idolatry, the Torah forbade Israel to erect a pillar (matzeva) for the service of God, because idol worshippers were accustomed to do so. It also forbade planting a tree near the Temple, or bowing down outside the Temple on a figured stone (even maskit), because that is what idol worshippers did (below 15:11-13). For Bnei Noaḥ, however, there is no prohibition in this; only things that are liable to lead them to actually worship idols are forbidden.<sup>6</sup></p><p>Israel were warned not to mention the name of an idol or to cause others to mention it, as it is stated (Shemot 23:13): \"And you shall not mention the name of other gods; it shall not be heard on your mouth\" (below 14:1), whereas for Bnei Noaḥ there is no prohibition in this (Ramban; SMA, ḤM 176:92).<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"The law of the mesit and his punishment applies to a Jew who enticed a Jew, but there is no law of mesit for Bnei Noaḥ, even if he enticed a Jew (Minḥat Ḥinukh 462:1). Some say that the law of mesit applies also to a ben Noaḥ who enticed a Jew (Ḥesed Li-meshiḥo on Megillat Rut, Kuntres Sho'el Ka-inyan; Maharam Schick, OḤ 229; Shem Mi-Shmuel of Salir [?], Devarim 25:17). In any case, according to all opinions a ben Noaḥ is forbidden to cause his fellow to sin and to draw him to idolatry, as with any transgression (see Sanhedrin 29a, regarding the enticement of the serpent)."},{"n":6,"en":"In Rosh Ha-shana 24b and Avoda Zara 43a it is related that Rabban Gamliel had images of the moon, for the purpose of receiving testimony for the sanctification of the month. It was asked: but it is forbidden to make images of the moon? And it was answered that non-Jews made the images for him. Tosafot asked (in both places, s.v. \"Shani\"): but something forbidden to a Jew is forbidden by rabbinic law, as a shevut, to ask a non-Jew to do for him? They answered that for the sake of a mitzva it is permitted to transgress a shevut. From their words it is understood that the non-Jews themselves are permitted to make these images.<br>On the other hand, however, Rambam wrote regarding a ben Noaḥ (Hilkhot Melakhim 9:2): \"And he is not allowed to erect a pillar, nor to plant an asheira, nor to make images and the like for decoration.\" Simply, it seems his intent is that if there is a concern that they will thereby stumble into idolatry, they are forbidden to do these things, and Israel too has a mitzva to instruct them so. But when there is no concern that they will thereby stumble into idolatry, because the idol worshippers there are not accustomed to this, there is no prohibition. Accordingly, Rambam does not disagree with Tosafot. Similarly, She'elat Shlomo 21 explained that according to Rambam a non-Jew is forbidden in all the prohibitions connected to idolatry, including the making of images that one might come to worship. He cited the words of Nir Le-David 168, in the gloss, that all the prohibitions of Bnei Noaḥ are derived by asmakhta (scriptural allusion), and consequently anything that seemed to the Sages connected to idolatry is included in the prohibition for Bnei Noaḥ. Binyan Shlomo (Sanhedrin 56b, s.v. \"Hineh\") wrote explicitly that according to Rambam, by rabbinic law Bnei Noaḥ are not allowed to make images lest they err and worship them, for Israel is commanded, when it is in their power, to prevent Bnei Noaḥ from sinning with idolatry. Similarly, Leḥem Mishneh (Hilkhot Melakhim 10:9) wrote that all the things that Rambam wrote they are not executed for are mitzvot by rabbinic law. And according to Avnei Nezer (YD 123:2), the mitzva is upon Israel, that when they are able, they should prevent Bnei Noaḥ from stumbling into idolatry. That is why [Rambam] changed his wording and wrote \"he is not allowed,\" whereas regarding worship for which one is not executed he wrote \"it is forbidden,\" because that prohibition is upon the non-Jews (see Toldot Noaḥ, pp. 138-139). In any case, the implication is that only when there is a concern that they will stumble into idolatry through the images is there a mitzva to prevent them. And this is what I wrote above.<br>Some understood Rambam to mean that the prohibition for Bnei Noaḥ applies in any case. According to Minḥat Ḥinukh 27:7, in Rambam's view this is a Torah mitzva, for it is not relevant that the Sages would forbid anything to them. According to this understanding, Tosafot disagree with Rambam, for if Rabban Gamliel asked them [to make the images], then according to Rambam he transgressed the prohibition of lifnei iver (placing a stumbling block before the blind) by causing Bnei Noaḥ to stumble. According to Rambam, one must explain as Rosh explained (Avoda Zara 3:5), that the non-Jews made the images of the moon for themselves, or as Ritva explained, that they made them on their own initiative for Rabban Gamliel."},{"n":7,"en":"Many wrote that Bnei Noaḥ are not forbidden to swear by idols, for this does not prove acceptance of their divinity (Aliyot De-Rabbeinu Yona, Sanhedrin 63b; Ramban, Ra'ah, Ran, Arukh La-ner there; Turei Even, Megilla 28a). Some say that thanking an idol for something good that happened is included in a declaration of belief in the idol and is forbidden (Taz 148:3), and some say that only a clear declaration of acceptance of the idol as a god is forbidden, but thanksgiving is not forbidden (Baḥ 148:7; Nekudot Ha-kesef)."}]},{"slug":"20-05-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-06/","title_he":"ו – היחס המיקל לכותים","title_en":"The Lenient Attitude toward the Kutim","body_en":"<p>After the king of Assyria exiled Israel from Shomron and brought the Kutim (Samaritans) in their place, lions would kill the Kutim. \"And they spoke to the king of Assyria, saying: The nations that you exiled and settled in the cities of Shomron do not know the law of the God of the land, and He has sent lions among them, and behold, they are killing them, because they do not know the law of the God of the land. And the king of Assyria commanded, saying: Bring there one of the priests whom you exiled from there, and let them go and dwell there, and let him teach them the law of the God of the land. So one of the priests whom they had exiled from Shomron came and dwelt in Beit El, and he taught them how they should fear the Lord\" (II Melakhim 17:26-28). \"So these nations feared the Lord, and they also served their graven images, both their children and their children's children\" (ibid. 17:41). And even though they associated the service of God with idolatry, the lions no longer killed them.</p><p>We learned two principles. The first: God is more exacting regarding idolatry in the Land of Israel than outside the Land, and therefore those Kutim who continued their practices in the Land were punished by the lions. The second: God is less exacting with Bnei Noaḥ than with Israel, and therefore as long as they combine faith in God with their idolatry, they are not punished.</p><p>Ramban explained that since God's providence over the nations is exercised indirectly, through angels and ministers appointed over them, He is not exacting with them when they err and worship those [powers] in their own lands. But in the Land of Israel, which is watched over by God, the exactingness toward them increases. Even so, they were not required to serve God alone as Israel are; rather, as long as they also feared God, even though they also worshipped idols, they were no longer punished (Vayikra 18:25; Derasha for Rosh Ha-shana; Recanati there; Responsa of Rashba Ha-ḥadashot 368; Rabbeinu Baḥya, Devarim 31:16; Alshikh, II Melakhim 17).</p><p>Radak expanded on this and explained (II Melakhim 17:28) that the priest who taught the Kutim did not try to prevent them from idolatry, because he knew they were incapable of it; rather, he taught them that the Lord is the God of gods, and thereby they were saved. Malbim added (there 28-41) that since Bnei Noaḥ did not stand in the covenant with God, they were not forbidden shituf, and therefore as long as they combine faith in God with their idolatry, their turning to God is desirable and they bear no sin.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"This explanation follows those who hold that the Kutim were considered non-Jews, at least in the first generations. See Peninei Halakha, Giyur 3:9, note 8, that there are Tanna'im and Amora'im who hold that the Kutim are not Jews at all, because they continued to worship idols and therefore their conversion was not valid. However, according to most Tanna'im and Amora'im, the Kutim were considered Jews. Tosafot explained (Yevamot 24b, s.v. \"Halakha\") that in their view, at first they were not considered Jews because they continued to worship idols, and only after generations did they convert fully and were considered Jews. On the other hand, many Rishonim hold that their conversion was valid from the outset, and by continuing to worship their idols they became apostate Jews. According to their view, one must explain that just as God is not exacting with non-Jews, so too with converts, until they are fully integrated into Israel. Therefore, by accepting to serve God as well, they were saved from the lions. This is similar to what Rabbi Yisrael Bassan wrote in Responsa Laḥmei Toda 5: just as a minor is not punished by Heaven until age twenty (Shabbat 89b), so a convert is not punished during the first twenty years after his conversion."}]},{"slug":"20-05-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-07/","title_he":"ז – עבודה זרה בשיתוף לישראל","title_en":"Idolatry in Shituf for Israel","body_en":"<p>Idolatry in shituf (association) means that a person believes that the Lord is the God of gods, the source of the powers and the One capable of influencing them, and therefore he prays to Him. Yet at the same time he believes that God created various powers that govern the world, such as the forces of nature, and that they have an independent ability to influence what happens in the world, to do good or to do harm. And since they do good to those who revere them and harm those who do not worship them, he bows down to their statues and performs rites before them. In this way he associates with the service of God and faith in Him also faith in idols and their worship.</p><p>As we learned (above 3:4), for Israel idolatry in shituf is forbidden like outright idolatry, as it is stated (Shemot 22:19): \"One who sacrifices to the gods, save to the Lord alone, shall be utterly destroyed.\" That is, a Jew who believes in God but also worshipped other gods besides God \"shall be utterly destroyed,\" meaning he shall be put to death. For the Torah commanded Israel regarding faith in God's unity, as it is stated (Devarim 6:4): \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is one.\" God likewise warned Israel in the Ten Commandments not to associate other idols in their faith, as it is stated (Shemot 20:2-5): \"You shall have no other gods before Me. You shall not make for yourself a graven image… You shall not bow down to them nor serve them, for I the Lord your God am a jealous God…\" Ramban explained (there) that this jealousy is directed specifically toward Israel, for Israel are His treasured people, whom He separated for Himself from all the peoples, as it is stated (Vayikra 20:26): \"And I have separated you from the peoples to be Mine.\" And it is stated (Shemot 19:6): \"And you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation.\" Therefore, when Israel worship idols, God is jealous against them \"as a man is jealous of his wife when she goes after others, and of his servant when he makes for himself another master.\"</p><p>This was the sin of Israel in the sin of the Golden Calf: they did not deny God and did not want to stop serving Him, but they associated idolatry as well. After they thought that Moshe Rabbeinu would not return from Mount Sinai, they reasoned that without him they could not be watched over directly by God, for He is too exalted, and in order to receive help they had to worship also one of the idols beneath Him. As it is stated (Shemot 32:1-4): \"And the people saw that Moshe delayed to come down from the mountain, and the people gathered against Aharon and said to him: Rise, make us gods who shall go before us, for this Moshe, the man who brought us up out of the land of Egypt, we do not know what has become of him… And all the people took off the golden rings that were in their ears and brought them to Aharon. And he took it from their hand and fashioned it with a graving tool and made it a molten calf, and they said: These are your gods, O Israel…\" And even though they continued to believe in God, since they associated idolatry, they became liable to death. Those who sinned before witnesses who had warned them were punished with death by the sword at the hands of the sons of Levi; those who sinned before witnesses who had not warned them died that same day in the plague with which God struck them; and all the rest were tested by drinking the water into which the calf had been ground, and the sinners died of a disease of the belly called dropsy (Yoma 66b; Rashi, Shemot 32:20).</p><p>However, the sin of Jews who worship idols in shituf is lighter than the sin of outright idol worshippers. As the Sages said, Israel did not become liable to annihilation for the sin of the Calf, because they continued to believe in God, as it is stated (Shemot 32:8): \"And they said: These are your gods, O Israel, who brought you up (he'elukha) out of the land of Egypt\" – he'elukha is in the plural, meaning [they included] God as well (Sanhedrin 63a). We likewise learn that the sin of idol worshippers who do not associate the service of God is graver, as it is stated (Shoftim 10:6): \"And the children of Israel again did evil in the eyes of the Lord, and served the Be'alim… and they forsook the Lord and did not serve Him.\" The Sages said (Eikha Rabba, Petiḥta 10): \"'And did not serve Him' – not even in partnership,\" and therefore they were punished with extra severity.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"See on all this at length above 3, note 2. Some say that a Jew who worshipped idols in shituf is liable to death, like for outright idolatry (Ve-shav Ha-kohen 38; Responsa of Rabbi Akiva Eiger, vol. 4, YD 43; Melamed Le-ho'il, OḤ 16). Others hold that he is not liable to death, since they said \"he is uprooted from the world\" and did not say he is liable to death (Arukh La-ner, Sanhedrin 67a; Meged Shamayim, YD 65; Yeḥaveh Da'at 10 [?]; Da Ma She-tashiv, Sha'ar 3, letter 4)."}]},{"slug":"20-05-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-08/","title_he":"ח – דעת המקילים בשיתוף בבני נח","title_en":"The View of Those Lenient about Shituf for Bnei Noaḥ","body_en":"<p>The poskim disagree whether idolatry in shituf is forbidden to Bnei Noaḥ. That is, the prohibition of idolatry is one of the seven mitzvot of Bnei Noaḥ (above section 1), and the question is whether it also includes a prohibition against worshipping idols in shituf. On this question also depends the attitude toward believers in Christianity, who hold that \"that man\" too is God.</p><p>According to most poskim and commentators, although it is fitting for Bnei Noaḥ to believe in the faith of God's unity, only the people of Israel are obligated in this, as explained in the previous section. But Bnei Noaḥ are not forbidden to worship idols in shituf: as long as they believe that the Lord is the God of gods and also turn to Him in prayer, even if they associate belief in an additional idol, despite the error in this, they do not transgress the prohibition of idolatry. Similarly, we learned that the Torah warned Israel to destroy every idolatrous statue and not to benefit from it, whereas Bnei Noaḥ are not warned about this (section 4). For as long as they associate faith in God, there is no prohibition for them in keeping the statues, and consequently they need not destroy them. We also learned (section 3) that a sacrifice is not accepted from a Jew who worshipped idols, whereas from a ben Noaḥ who worshipped idols a sacrifice is accepted: since he comes to offer to God, he is associating faith in God, and he does not have the status of an idol worshipper. We also learned that Israel must give up their lives for faith in God's unity, whereas Bnei Noaḥ are not obligated in this (section 2). This is because as long as they also believe in God, they are not considered idol worshippers, and consequently need not give up their lives. Similarly, we learned that Israel are forbidden to make statues of angels, heavenly bodies and people for decoration, whereas for a ben Noaḥ there is no prohibition (section 5): since they are not forbidden to associate, as long as they also believe in God, they are not forbidden to make statues.</p><p>We also learn this from the fact that the Torah repeatedly warned Israel in a special way regarding faith in God's unity, as it is stated (Devarim 4:9-20): \"Only take heed to yourself and guard your soul diligently, lest you forget the things your eyes have seen, and lest they depart from your heart all the days of your life, and make them known to your children and your children's children. The day that you stood before the Lord your God at Ḥorev… Take good heed therefore of yourselves, for you saw no manner of form on the day that the Lord spoke to you at Ḥorev out of the midst of the fire, lest you deal corruptly and make yourselves a graven image… and lest you lift up your eyes to the heavens, and when you see the sun and the moon and the stars, all the host of heaven, you be drawn away and bow down to them and serve them, which the Lord your God has allotted to all the peoples under the whole heaven. But the Lord has taken you and brought you out of the iron furnace, out of Egypt, to be to Him a people of inheritance, as at this day.\" The Rishonim explained that since God did not reveal Himself to the nations of the world as He revealed Himself to Israel at the Sinai revelation, His governance of the world is reflected to them through the forces of nature, \"which the Lord your God has allotted to all the peoples under the whole heaven.\" And since God bestows abundance upon each people and each land through stars, constellations and ministering powers, they are liable to attribute independent powers to them and to worship them (Rashbam, Ramban, Rashba and others). Most Aḥaronim learned from this that as long as they also believe in God as the supreme God, there is no prohibition in their associating belief in other idols as well.</p><p>The aspiration, however, is to purify faith of every trace of idolatry, and so it will be in the future, as it is stated (Zekharia 14:9): \"And the Lord shall be King over all the earth; on that day the Lord shall be one and His Name one.\" But as long as the moral and spiritual situation is not suited to this, there is a concern that if Bnei Noaḥ do not believe at all, their moral situation may be worse. Therefore it is better that they believe in shituf, and thereby strengthen themselves against evil inclinations and murderousness, and progress gradually in the levels of faith and morality (see below chapter 9).<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"Devarim 4:19: \"which the Lord your God has allotted to all the peoples under the whole heaven.\" Rashbam explained: \"According to the primary plain meaning: which He left for all the peoples to worship, for He is not concerned about them; but you the Lord took and brought out to be to Him a people of inheritance and to serve Him, and He will be your God.\" Ibn Ezra: \"For each and every people has a known star and constellation, and likewise there is a constellation for each and every city.\" Ramban: \"For all of them have a star and a constellation, and above them are supernal angels, such as what is said in Daniel: the prince of the kingdom of Persia and the prince of the kingdom of Greece; and because of this they make gods for themselves of them and worship them…\" Admittedly, they did not write explicitly that Bnei Noaḥ are not forbidden idolatry in shituf, but many Aḥaronim understood this to be their view, as Ha-ketav Ve-ha-kabbala explained at length (Devarim 4:19). And Rashba wrote explicitly (Responsa Ha-ḥadashot from manuscript, 368): \"For the Blessed Name divided the lands among the constellations of the heavens and set their dominion on earth, so that such-and-such a star governs such-and-such a place; therefore they divided in their lands, in their places, in belief: this one worships such-and-such a form, and that one worships another form. And one who worships that star which governs that place is not like an idol worshipper, provided that he knows and recognises that the power and dominion of that star come only from the Blessed Name, who appointed it governor in that land. This is like what is said about the Kutim (II Melakhim 17:33): 'They feared the Lord and served their own gods…'\"<br>Many Aḥaronim likewise wrote that idolatry in shituf is not forbidden to non-Jews. We shall mention some of those who explain the spiritual reason for this: Rav Moshe Zacut, cited in Mikdash Melekh (Ha'azinu, p. 106) by Rav Shalom Buzaglo; Rav Binyamin Ha-kohen Vitale (Gevul Binyamin 1:39); Rav Pinḥas Horowitz (Panim Yafot, Bereishit 11:1; 31:53; Vayikra 9:6; and elsewhere); Rav Binyamin Ze'ev Boskowitz in Seder Mishneh (Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-torah 1:7; Hilkhot Avoda Zara 3:3); Rav Eliezer Horowitz (No'am Megadim, Parashat Bamidbar, s.v. \"Ve-zeh yirmoz\"; many cited his words); Rabbi Levi Yitzḥak of Berditchev (Kedushat Levi, Va'etḥanan 5:7); Rav Ḥayim Tirer of Czernowitz (Be'er Mayim Ḥayim, Shemot 20:2); Rav Yosef Shaul Nathanson (Sho'el U-meshiv, Tinyana 1:51; Divrei Sha'ul, first part, Vayelekh, s.v. \"Va-yomer\"; third part, Vayeḥi, s.v. \"Hikavtzu\"; and he returned to expand on this many times in his books); Maharatz Chajes (Torat Ha-nevi'im 11); Malbim (II Melakhim 17:34, and elsewhere); our master Rav Kook: \"And Bnei Noaḥ are not warned regarding shituf, which is [due to] their inability to grasp [pure faith] in their imagination and spiritual power\" (Shemona Kevatzim 8:44); \"Shituf is for them, for the time being, the culmination of ascent\" (Orot, Yisrael U-teḥiyato 5); Rav Ḥarlap (Mei Marom 10:35; 12:32:2); Rav Avraham Binyamin Silberberg (Hadrat Melekh on Rambam's Sefer Ha-mitzvot 2); Rav Price in Mishnat Avraham (on Sefer Ḥasidim, vol. 3, siman 1454). This is also the view of the Chabad Rebbes: the Tzemaḥ Tzedek (Derekh Mitzvotekha, the mitzva of God's unity; he wrote that this is the view of Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 1:1-2), and the seventh Rebbe, Rabbi Menaḥem Mendel Schneerson (Likutei Siḥot, vol. 20, p. 16, note 44). Below, in note 12, many more poskim will be cited who wrote this as halakha regarding Christian Bnei Noaḥ, that they are not considered idol worshippers because they believe in shituf."}]},{"slug":"20-05-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-09/","title_he":"ט – דעת המחמירים בשיתוף וביחס לנוצרים","title_en":"The View of Those Stringent about Shituf, and regarding Christians","body_en":"<p>On the other hand, some hold that just as idolatry in shituf is forbidden to Israel like outright idolatry, so it is forbidden to non-Jews. True, we learned that the Torah distanced Israel from idolatry and added commands for them in which Bnei Noaḥ are not commanded; however, in the essential prohibition of idolatry, Israel and Bnei Noaḥ are equal. For the Sages stated a rule (Sanhedrin 56b): \"Regarding idolatry, the things for which a Jewish court executes – a ben Noaḥ is warned about them.\" And since a Jew who worshipped idols in shituf is liable to death, as it is stated (Shemot 22:19): \"One who sacrifices to the gods, save to the Lord alone, shall be utterly destroyed,\" Bnei Noaḥ too are forbidden to worship idols in shituf, and one who worships in shituf is liable to death.</p><p>They further argued that if Bnei Noaḥ were permitted to worship idols in shituf, the prohibition of idolatry would be nullified for them, since all idol worshippers believe in some way in a primordial Creator who is above everything. As it is stated (Malakhi 1:11): \"For from the rising of the sun to its setting My Name is great among the nations, and in every place incense is offered to My Name, and a pure offering, for My Name is great among the nations, says the Lord of hosts.\" The Sages explained that the meaning is that in all places, even the idol worshippers call God \"the God of gods\" (Menaḥot 110a), and nevertheless they are called idol worshippers (Me'il Tzedaka 22). Those who permit respond that if they hold that the primordial Creator does not influence the world, and they do not turn to Him in prayer, they are considered outright idol worshippers. But if they believe that the God of gods too influences the world and they also turn to Him in prayer, they are considered worshippers of idolatry in shituf.</p><p>Those who are stringent further derived that idolatry in shituf is forbidden to non-Jews from the fact that the Sages forbade doing business with Christians on Sunday and the three days before it, as with idol worshippers, even though Christians associate the Name of Heaven. True, the Rishonim permitted doing business with Christians in practice, but their main reason was that Christians are not devout, and because of [the risk of] enmity and the need for a livelihood, and not because they do not have the status of idol worshippers. Therefore the stringent poskim ruled that idolatry in shituf is forbidden to non-Jews, and Christians are considered idol worshippers.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The Sages said regarding Christians that Sunday is their festival day, and therefore it is forbidden to trade with a Christian on Sunday and the three days before it, lest because of his success in trade he give thanks to his god (below 11:4). And according to Rabbi Yishmael, who forbids trading also on the three days after their festival day, \"a Christian is always forbidden\" (Avoda Zara 6a, in the version that preceded censorship). So ruled Rambam (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 9:4; Commentary on the Mishna, Avoda Zara 3:2). What was permitted in practice is not because idolatry in shituf is permitted to them, but because they are not devout, or because of a great need for livelihood and prevention of enmity (below 11, note 5). True, Rabbeinu Tam permitted making Christians swear an oath in business matters, since there is no prohibition in causing them to swear in shituf (Sanhedrin 63b, s.v. \"Asur\"; Bekhorot 2b, s.v. \"Shema\"), and so wrote Rosh, Agudda and Rabbi Yitzḥak Almandari on Sanhedrin there, and so wrote Semag (positive commandments 82); Hagahot Maimoniyot (Hilkhot Sheluḥin 5:10); and Rabbeinu Yeruḥam. However, Rav Shmuel Landau, son of the Noda Bi-Yehuda, wrote (Noda Bi-Yehuda Tinyana, YD 148) that the permission is only to make them swear in shituf, but idolatry in shituf is forbidden to Bnei Noaḥ.<br>So wrote Rav Efrayim Ha-kohen (Sha'ar Efrayim 24), that Bnei Noaḥ are forbidden idolatry in shituf, for the rule is that every prohibition for which a Jew is liable to death, a ben Noaḥ too is warned about (Sanhedrin 56b), and Jews are liable to death for idolatry in shituf (see above note 9). So wrote Rav Yona Landsofer (Me'il Tzedaka 22), who disagreed with the lenient opinions with various arguments, among them that in practice every idol worshipper also associates the Name of Heaven, since all believe in the one source, as cited above. So wrote Rav Refael Ha-kohen (Ve-shav Ha-kohen 38); Rav Shmuel Kelin (Maḥatzit Ha-shekel, OḤ 156:1); Rav Yosef Te'omim (Pri Megadim, Siftei Da'at, YD 65:11, and elsewhere); Rabbi Akiva Eiger (Responsa, vol. 4, YD 43); Rav Tzvi Hirsch Kalischer (Emuna Yeshara, vol. 2, 7); Rav Yosef Babad (Minḥat Ḥinukh 86:2); Rav David Friedman (She'ilat David, end of vol. 1, on Bekhorot); Rav Shlomo Yehuda Tabak (Teshurat Shai 1:390); Rav David Ha-levi Horowitz (Imrei David 181). Inclining to this view were Rav Yom Tov Lipmann Heller (Pilpula Ḥarifta on Rosh, Sanhedrin 7:3, letter 5) and Rav Avraham Yitzḥak Glick (Yad Yitzḥak 3:15). However, the stringent authorities knew that the prevalent position follows the lenient view, as Sha'ar Efrayim wrote (24): \"I have seen many who err in this explanation, saying that a ben Noaḥ is not warned about shituf\"; and likewise Rav Shmuel Landau (Noda Bi-Yehuda Tinyana, YD 148): \"And although this matter is commonly said by several scholars, that non-Jews are not commanded regarding shituf, and several books of sermons and aggadot also made use of this premise\"; and likewise Rav David Friedman wrote (She'ilat David, end of vol. 1): \"I have elaborated on this because there are many who err in this, permitting the worship of shituf to Bnei Noaḥ.\""}]},{"slug":"20-05-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-10/","title_he":"י – דעת המקילים ומעמד הנוצרים וההינדים","title_en":"The Lenient View and the Status of Christians and Hindus","body_en":"<p>The view of most poskim, as halakha and in practice, is that non-Jews are not forbidden to worship idols in shituf. Therefore Christian non-Jews are not considered idol worshippers, since together with their belief in \"that man\" they believe in God. It seems that the same law applies to adherents of the Hindu religion, who in principle associate [with their gods] faith in a supreme God (see below 8:5, and at the end of the note here).<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"The view of Rema in Darkhei Moshe (OḤ 156:2) and in his gloss on the SA there is that Christians are not idol worshippers, since they associate the Name of Heaven, and so wrote Shakh (YD 151:7). Among the Rishonim, we find that they permitted doing business with Christians because they are not warned against swearing in shituf; this is the view of Rabbeinu Tam as cited in Tosafot (Sanhedrin 63b, s.v. \"Asur\"); Rosh, Agudda and Rabbi Yitzḥak Almandari (Sanhedrin there); Semag (positive commandments 82); Hagahot Maimoniyot (Hilkhot Sheluḥin 5:10). According to the lenient opinions there is no difference between an oath in shituf and idolatry in shituf. So wrote Rabbeinu Yeruḥam (Toldot Adam Ve-Ḥava 17:5): \"For Bnei Noaḥ were not warned regarding shituf.\" Moreover, several of those who hold that Bnei Noaḥ were not warned regarding shituf explained that all the leniencies of the Rishonim permitting business with Christians (below 11:5, note 5) are based on the principle that Christianity is idolatry in shituf and is not forbidden to Bnei Noaḥ. And although they added further considerations such as enmity and livelihood, the basis for relying on those considerations is that they are not warned regarding shituf (Zera Emet 2:112; Rav Yosef Eliyahu Henkin in Teshuvot Ivra 115; Mishnat Avraham on Semag, vol. 1, negative commandment 45, 106; Aseh Lekha Rav 9:30; and others). As for what was said (Sanhedrin 56b): \"Regarding idolatry, the things for which a Jewish court executes – a ben Noaḥ is warned about them,\" this pertains only to one who worships outright idolatry: then, if he performed a rite for which a Jew is liable to death, he too is warned about it and is liable to death.<br>A large majority of the Aḥaronim likewise ruled that Christianity is not forbidden to Bnei Noaḥ, and therefore Christians are not considered idol worshippers. Among them: Rav Moshe Rivkes (Be'er Ha-gola, ḤM 425:1); Rav Yair Bacharach (Ḥavot Yair 185); Rav Alexander Schor (Tevu'ot Shor 4:1); Rav Binyamin Ze'ev Boskowitz explained this at length (Seder Mishneh on Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-torah 1:7:1-3; Hilkhot Avoda Zara 3:3); Rav Elazar Fleckeles (Teshuva Me-ahava 1:69); Rav Shlomo Zalman Lifshitz (Ḥemdat Shlomo, OḤ 36:14); Rav Yaakov Orenstein (Yeshu'ot Yaakov, OḤ 156:1, where he disagreed on this with his grandfather, the Sha'ar Efrayim); Rav Avraham Ha-kohen of Salonika (Shiyurei Tahara 200:6); Rav Ḥayim Palagi (Ḥayim Va-melekh, Hilkhot Melakhim 9:2), and so wrote in his name his son Rav Avraham Palagi (Shema Avraham 2:51); Rav Yaakov Ettlinger (Binyan Tziyon 1:63); Rav Yosef Shaul Nathanson, who expanded on this greatly (Sho'el U-meshiv, Tinyana 1:51; Telita'a 1:55; and in many other places in his responsa and in his books on the Torah); Rav Yekutiel Yehuda Teitelbaum (Avnei Tzedek, YD 105); Rav Yaakov Tenenbaum (Naharei Afarsemon, YD 114); Rav Ze'ev Turobowitz (Tiferet Ziv, vol. 2, YD 22:6); Rav Raḥamim Franco (Sha'arei Raḥamim 5); Rav David Tzvi Hoffmann (Melamed Le-ho'il, YD 55); Rav Mordekhai Horowitz (Mateh Levi, vol. 2, YD 28); Rav Yitzḥak Unna (Sho'alin Ve-dorshin 35); Rav Yeḥezkel Lifschitz (Ha-midrash Ve-ha-ma'aseh, Noaḥ); Rav Gershon Stern (Yalkut Gershuni, YD 147:2-3); Rav Barukh Ha-levi Epstein (Torah Temima, Shemot 23:13, note 91); Rav Yitzḥak Isaac Ha-levi Herzog (Teḥuka Le-Yisrael, vol. 1, 2:6); Rav Reuven Margaliot (Tal Teḥiya, \"Mishpetei Ger Toshav\"); Rav Yosef Eliyahu Henkin (Kitvei Ha-Gria Henkin, vol. 2, p. 226); Rav Avraham Aharon Price likewise explained this at length, and wrote that a ger toshav is one who has accepted upon himself not to worship idols even in shituf (Mishnat Avraham, vol. 2, 1:1-2, and likewise in Mishnat Avraham on Semag, vol. 1, negative commandment 1:8-9; negative commandment 20:10); Rav Avraham Binyamin Silberberg (Hadrat Melekh on Rambam's Sefer Ha-mitzvot 2); Rav Ḥayim David Halevi (Aseh Lekha Rav 8:68); Rav Shalom Messas (Shemesh U-magen, vol. 3, OḤ 30-31). This is in addition to the Rishonim and Aḥaronim mentioned above in note 10, who gave a spiritual reason why shituf was not forbidden to Bnei Noaḥ, among them: Rashba; Rav Moshe Zacut; Rav Pinḥas Horowitz in Panim Yafot; Rav Levi Yitzḥak of Berditchev; the Tzemaḥ Tzedek; Malbim; Rav Kook; Rav Ḥarlap. From what we have learned, we find that more than a hundred of the great rabbis wrote this position, while the number of those who are stringent is about a third of that.<br>Rav Shmuel ben Yosef explained (Olat Tamid on Rema, OḤ 156:3) that the meaning of shituf is that they believe that God is above the idol, and then they do not \"impair the unity of the Blessed Name.\" So wrote Sho'el U-meshiv (Tinyana 1:51). It seems to me, in my humble opinion, that all the lenient authorities agree with this, for if they associate faith in God, who is abstract and omnipotent, it is self-evident that God is above.<br><b>The Hindu religion</b>: some wrote that it is outright idolatry since it is saturated with idols (see Mar'eh Ha-bazak 6:61), and some wrote that it is idolatry in shituf (Rav Eliyahu Benamozegh, Israel and Humanity, p. 74). This is also the view of Rav Prof. Daniel Sperber, who investigated this issue thoroughly and at length, and likewise Rav Yissakhar Heiman in his book \"Judaism and Hinduism.\" This is also my conclusion, based on what I have learned about the Hindu religion, and as explained below (8:4-5), based on the central relationship to the supreme root called \"Brahman,\" which is infinite and the soul of the entire universe, while the various gods are manifestations of it. True, at first only a minority were considered to be associating [faith in a supreme God], but as part of the process of world elevation, today it can already be said that the principle of belief in a supreme God is agreed upon, and there is no one unaware of the supreme root called \"Brahman.\" And even if there are those who are not careful to direct their prayer to it as well, they do not reject the explanation of their sages that their prayer is also directed to it; rather, they rely on them and defer their own opinion to theirs. And although most of the discussions about shituf were stated in relation to Christians, in practice the principle explained in note 10 applies to them [Hindus] as well. This also emerges from the words of our master Rav Kook, who did not hold that adherents of idolatrous religions are obligated to convert to Christianity or Islam, since they can refine their faith within the framework of their own religion (see below 9:6)."}]},{"slug":"20-05-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-11/","title_he":"יא – השינוי ביחס לנוצרים ומעמד המינים","title_en":"The Change in Attitude toward Christians, and the Status of the Minim","body_en":"<p>But we must explain why the Sages in the Talmud considered Christians idol worshippers, whereas in practice many of the Aḥaronim ruled that idolatry in shituf is permitted to Bnei Noaḥ, and therefore Christianity is not considered idolatry for them.</p><p>Three explanations can be offered, which combine with and complement one another. But before we explain them, let us preface that even one who does not accept these arguments will need to find another explanation for the fact that the Sages of the Talmud considered Christianity outright idolatry, whereas most poskim ruled that Christianity is idolatry in shituf and permitted to Bnei Noaḥ, and some even hold that it is not idolatry at all, as cited in the next section.</p><p>The first explanation: at first many Christians were Jews or converts, and according to all opinions shituf is forbidden to Jews like outright idolatry; therefore there was no room to be lenient in their case on the basis that they associate the Name of Heaven.</p><p>The second: since Christianity turned to idol worshippers, those who became Christians revered mainly \"that man\" and the statues they saw in churches. And although the Tanakh was the foundational book of their religion, and their scholars also turned to God who revealed Himself to the prophets of Israel, since almost all Christians, including some of the monks, could not read, their reverence was mainly for the statues of \"that man,\" like all idol worshippers who revered statues, and therefore they were considered idol worshippers. Over time, in a gradual process, more and more Christians rose to associate the Name of Heaven, that is, to emphasise also faith in God as explained in the Tanakh. In parallel, we find many of the great Rishonim who raised the argument to be lenient regarding Christians, that they are worshippers of idolatry in shituf. But since the faith of the Christian masses still remained essentially idolatrous, and their reverence was for the statues of \"that man,\" they did not decide to regard them as worshippers of idolatry in shituf. Later, many learned to read, and their reading of the Tanakh intensified their faith in God, and more Christians began to direct their worship to God, who forbade idolatry and the making of statues. As part of this process, in the year 5277 (1517), the Protestant stream crystallised within Christianity; it removed all the statues from the churches and broke them, leaving only the crosses as an image to which they bow. In parallel, in the Catholic stream too, which left the statues in place, faith in God as explained in the Tanakh intensified, and Christian scholars explained that the statues are symbols. Following this, in the period of the Aḥaronim it was ruled that Christianity is idolatry in shituf, and since most rabbis ruled that Bnei Noaḥ are not forbidden to worship idols in shituf, they regarded Christians as not being idol worshippers.</p><p>The third: since Christianity emerged from within Judaism and challenged the foundations of the faith and Torah of Israel, it posed an especially grave threat to Judaism, and therefore the Sages defined it as minut (heresy), from which one must keep away as from outright idolatry. Therefore, even when it was clear that the people in question were non-Jews who associated the Name of Heaven in their worship, they were stringent and regarded them as idol worshippers. But after many generations, when Christianity had moved away from Judaism and it was already clear that it was a different religion, and the vast majority of its believers were entirely non-Jewish, they returned to the basic law that Bnei Noaḥ are not forbidden to worship idols in shituf, and consequently they did not regard Christians who associated the Name of Heaven as idol worshippers. And as long as they did not try to convert Jews to Christianity, they did not regard them as minim.<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"Minim are holders of various views that threaten the existence of the Jewish religion. There were many groups of minim, as Rabbi Yoḥanan said (Yerushalmi, Sanhedrin 10:5): \"Israel did not go into exile until they had become twenty-four sects of minim.\" What all the minim had in common was that they knew the faith of Israel and incited Israel away from their faith. Therefore Rabbi Tarfon said (Shabbat 116b) that a Torah scroll written by minim must be burned, whereas a Torah scroll written by an idol worshipper should be put away in a geniza, because minim are worse than idol worshippers: \"for these know [God] and deny, while those do not know and deny.\" And so is the halakha (SA YD 281:1). The most widespread minim were the first Christians, who were Jews who fought Judaism and conducted propaganda to draw Jews to their religion and endangered the existence of Judaism; therefore the Sages often meant them by the term minim (Rashi, Megilla 24b; Piskei Rid, Ḥullin 13a; Ramban, Avoda Zara 27b; Nimukei Yosef, Avoda Zara 17a; Radbaz from manuscript, vol. 8, 191). It was mainly against them that the blessing against the minim (Birkat Ha-minim) was instituted in the Amida prayer, as Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Tefilla 2:1): \"In the days of Rabban Gamliel the minim multiplied in Israel, and they would oppress Israel and incite them to turn away from God.\" But sometimes they also defined as minim haters of Israel from among the Sadducees, the Boethusians and the Karaites (Sefer Yuḥasin, first essay). And sometimes they defined as books of minim the books of Aristotle and his disciples, who interwove into their words of wisdom denial of God and His providence (Riaz, Kuntres Ha-re'ayot, Sanhedrin 90a; Rambam, Hilkhot Teshuva 3:7; Responsa Rivash 45; Ra'av [Bartenura] and Penei Moshe, Sanhedrin 10:1; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav, Hilkhot Talmud Torah 3:7; and others; see below 13:6). Apparently, when these books were very dangerous, they too were included in the definition of \"books of minim.\""}]},{"slug":"20-05-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-12/","title_he":"יב – הסוברים שהנצרות אינה עבודה זרה","title_en":"Those Who Hold That Christianity Is Not Idolatry","body_en":"<p>Some say that Christianity is not idolatry at all, not even in shituf, for Christian scholars repeatedly and emphatically claim that they believe in the faith of God's unity as explained in the Torah. According to them, the Trinity in which they believe is nothing but three manifestations of the one God, and one of the three manifestations is through \"that man.\" And since, according to them, belief in the Trinity and in \"that man\" does not nullify faith in God's unity, then even though this involves a great distortion and an internal contradiction, such a belief is nevertheless not considered idolatry. Similarly regarding the statues and crosses: since according to Christian scholars divinity is not embodied in them, but they are only symbols, they are not considered idolatrous statues and images.</p><p>However, according to most poskim the law does not depend on a precise clarification of the view of Christian scholars, but on what the Christian masses believe and do. And since in practice the Christian masses, including priests, believe that \"that man\" is actually God, and bow down to images out of belief that divinity is embodied in them, they therefore regarded Christianity as idolatry in shituf.<sup>14</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"It must be emphasised that even according to those who hold that Christianity is not idolatry for Bnei Noaḥ, the prohibition for a Jew to convert to Christianity is as grave as idolatry, such that he must be killed rather than transgress; therefore he is obligated to give up his life rather than convert to Christianity or Islam (above 4:7). But regarding the definition of Christianity itself for Bnei Noaḥ, Christian scholars claim emphatically that their faith is in one God, and therefore they are called monotheists, and the Trinity is nothing but three manifestations of the one God, one of which is through \"that man.\" As Rihal (Rabbi Yehuda Ha-levi) reported in their name (Kuzari 1:4): \"Behold, we truly affirm the unity of God, even though the Trinity appears in our language.\" On this basis Meiri, who lived in Provence about 750 years ago (5009-5076), wrote in his commentary on Gittin (62a) that although Christians err in the belief in the Trinity, they are not considered idol worshippers, not even in shituf. So too held the disciple of Maharam Ḥalawa, Rav Ḥayim Galipapa (two generations after Meiri), that Christians are close \"to being gerei toshav, for they do not worship idols, and they believe in the verse that says: 'The Lord is our God, the Lord is one,' and likewise in 'You shall have no other gods,' etc., 'You shall not make for yourself a graven image,' etc. And the matter of the Trinity is not idolatry; rather, divinity is not properly received by them…\" (published in Or Yisrael, Monsey, 56). Similarly wrote Rashbatz, who lived in Algeria about six hundred years ago (5121-5204): he cited the words of Rihal and wrote (Magen Avot 2:4; 5:5) that \"that man\" apparently did not hold that he was a god, but later the believers in Christianity erred and regarded him as a god, and by the Trinity they deny the true unity; but the implication is that they are not actually worshippers of idolatry in shituf. So wrote Rav Yitzḥak Lampronti (5439-5517) in his book Paḥad Yitzḥak (entry \"Goy,\" p. 7a), that \"they are not idol worshippers, and they believe in the unity of God.\" So wrote Rav Makhluf Eldaudi (5585-5670, in Responsa Yisa Ish, YD 2, p. 20), that it is permitted to rent an apartment in Akko to Christians, and although they \"believe in three,\" \"behold, when you press them on this, they will answer that everything goes back to the unity of the Blessed God, for He is the first and the Rock of all.\" So wrote Rav Avraham Palagi (Shema Avraham 2:51): \"We hold that most non-Jews in our time do not worship idols and believe in His Blessed unity and in our Torah.\" This is also the view of Rav Sa'adya Amor and Rav Ḥayim Bleiḥ (cited in Responsa Mayim Kedoshim 66); Rav David Ha-kohen Skali (Kiryat Ḥana David, vol. 1, YD 16), Rav Barukh Avraham Toledano (She'alu Le-Varukh, vol. 1, YD 19) and his son Rav Pinḥas Toledano (in his notes \"Tosefet Berakha\" there). This is also the view of Rav Yosef Messas (5652-5734), that Christians in our time are not considered idol worshippers, not even in shituf (Mayim Kedoshim 63). Nevertheless, he absolutely forbade learning from their customs, since they contain a trace of idolatry remaining from what they learned from idolatrous rites (see Mayim Ḥayim 1:81; 1:298).<br>This is also the view of Rav Naḥum Eliezer Rabinovitch: \"Christians today no longer truly believe in more than one God, and although they pray to other things, these are only in the category of intermediaries (their messiah and the like), and therefore they are not defined as idol worshippers…\" (in Mar'eh Ha-bazak 8:35, note 12). And there were poskim who did not decide so, but added as a supporting consideration that Christians do not worship idols, but merely follow the custom of their fathers, and do not believe that there is divinity in the images; among them is Radbaz (8:117).<br>The great question for all who hold this view is why the Sages (Avoda Zara 6a) regarded Christians as idol worshippers and Sunday as their festival day. Apparently, even those who hold that Christianity is not idolatry agree that at first it contained shituf of idolatry, but over time it moved further and further from the idolatrous aspect, and in parallel those who hold that it is not idolatry multiplied (as explained in the previous section). Meiri (Avoda Zara 2a) has a unique explanation, according to which the Sages' intent in saying \"Notzrim\" was to adherents of an ancient idolatrous religion who worshipped the sun, to whom the prophet Yirmiyahu referred (4:16): \"Make mention to the nations, behold, proclaim against Jerusalem: watchers (notzrim) come from a far country and give out their voice against the cities of Yehuda.\" They were called \"Notzrim after Nebuchadnezzar (Nevukhadnetzar), and it is known that the image of the sun was in Babylon, and that all the people of Nebuchadnezzar worshipped it. And you already know that the sun serves on the first day [of the week]… and because of this they would call that day 'Notzri,' since it was fixed for Nebuchadnezzar…\"<br>However, according to the overwhelming majority of poskim, since Christians still claim that \"that man\" is God, they associate idolatry in their faith. There were, however, Christians who did not hold that he was God. Thus Rasag (Rav Sa'adya Gaon; Emunot Ve-de'ot 2:7) divided the Christians into four sects, one of which is not idolatry. Perhaps he meant the Arian stream, after Arius, who held that \"that man\" is not at the level of divinity of \"the Father.\" (His position was sharply rejected at the Council of Nicaea in 325, and over time the Arian stream disappeared.) Today there is the Unitarian stream, which believes that \"that man\" is not God but a messenger, and according to all opinions there is no idolatry in it. But Christians who hold that \"that man\" is God are considered, according to the overwhelming majority of poskim, worshippers of idolatry in shituf. The Protestant stream too, which crystallised from the year 5277 (1517), although it rejected statues, is not considered to have a different status from the Catholic one, since it did not change the principles of Christian belief in \"that man.\""}]},{"slug":"20-05-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-05-13/","title_he":"יג – הסוברים שנוצרים הגונים במעמד 'גר תושב'","title_en":"Those Who Hold That Decent Christians Have the Status of Ger Toshav","body_en":"<p>Rav Menaḥem Ha-Meiri explained two principles regarding Christians: first, that Christianity is not considered idolatry in shituf but a faith in God containing severe distortions, as we learned in the previous section. Second, that peoples who conduct themselves according to the ways of morality and decency, and have established laws for that purpose, are considered gerei toshav who keep the seven mitzvot of Bnei Noaḥ, and there is a mitzva to honour them and help them as much as possible.</p><p>With the first principle the vast majority of poskim did not agree. But with the second principle many of the great Aḥaronim of Israel agreed, and in their view, since Bnei Noaḥ are not forbidden to worship idols in shituf, Christianity is not forbidden to them; consequently, if they conduct themselves according to the customs of morality and decency, their status is that of gerei toshav, whom there is a mitzva to honour and help. So wrote our master Rav Kook: \"that the main view is like the opinion of Meiri, that all the peoples who are bounded by decent conventions between man and his fellow are already considered gerei toshav in all human obligations\" (Igrot Ha-Re'aya 89).<sup>15</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":15,"en":"Rav Menaḥem ben Shlomo Ha-Meiri of Provence (5009-5076, 1249-1316) established in his commentaries on the Talmud (Avoda Zara 2a; 20a; 26a; Gittin 62a; Bava Kamma 113a-b; Bava Metzia 27a) two principles: 1) Christians, despite the distortions in their religion, are not considered idol worshippers (as cited and quoted in the previous note). 2) Peoples bounded by religion and morality are considered as keeping the seven mitzvot of Bnei Noaḥ, and their law is like that of a ger toshav, whom there is a mitzva to save and sustain (Avoda Zara 26a, s.v. \"Ha-goyim\"; Bava Kamma 37b). His words are cited in Shita Mekubetzet (Bava Kamma 113a). Some hold that Meiri's words were said out of fear of the Christians, in order to save Israel, for on the basis of the words of the Talmud the Christian rulers persecuted the Jews and burned the Talmud. However, the vast majority of the Aḥaronim understood that this is truly his view.<br>As we learned in the previous section, the overwhelming majority of poskim did not agree with the first principle in Meiri's words, but many agreed with the second principle, according to which peoples who associate idolatry with their faith in God have their status determined by their moral state, and as long as they are bounded by the ways of morality, their law is that of a ger toshav. So wrote Rav Tzvi Hirsch Chajes (Ma'amar Tiferet Le-Yisrael 22:2) regarding all the laws of ger toshav; Rav Yoel Ashkenazi (Responsa Mahari Ashkenazi, YD 39, regarding evading customs duties); Maharsham 5:45; Rav David Tzvi Hoffmann (Ha-Shulḥan Arukh Ve-ha-rabbanim al Yaḥas Yisrael La-datot Aḥerot); Rav David Pipano (Ḥoshen Ha-efod 1:27, regarding a non-Jew's mistake); Rav Eliyahu Klatzkin (Imrei Shefer 92, regarding a lost object and a mistake); Rav Meir Arik (Minḥat Pitim, ḤM 266, regarding a lost object); Rav Yehuda Leib Graubart (Yamin U-smol, vol. 2, ch. 19, p. 227, regarding a lost object and a mistake); our master Rav Kook (Igrot Ha-Re'aya 89); Rav Yosef Eliyahu Henkin (Perushei Lev Ivra, p. 116, regarding their honour and property); Rav Yeḥiel Yaakov Weinberg (Li-frakim, 5763, p. 275); Tzitz Eliezer (15:47:5, regarding giving favour [to non-Jews]); Rav Shlomo Zalman Posner of Warsaw (regarding a lost object and a mistake); Rav Rabinovitch in Melumdei Milḥama 1:43. Many other Aḥaronim likewise wrote that the law of Christians in our day is like that of a ger toshav without mentioning Meiri, among them: Be'er Ha-gola (ḤM 266:1, regarding a lost object); Rav Yisrael Lipschitz (Tiferet Yisrael, Bava Kamma 4:3, Boaz 1, regarding their property); Rav Elazar Loew (Pekudat Elazar 29, regarding healing them); Rav Aryeh Leib Broda of Lvov (Mitzpeh Aryeh, Tinyana, siman 10, regarding healing a non-Jew on Shabbat); Rav Barukh Epstein (Torah Temima, Mishpatim 277, regarding their property and that their law is like that of Israel)."}]}]});
